│ The core issue is a change in Django’s       │
│ behavior regarding primary key values when   │
│ saving model instances. In Django 2.2 and    │
│ earlier, saving a model instance with an     │
│ explicit primary key would result in an      │
│ INSERT followed by an UPDATE. However, in    │
│ Django 3.0, this results in two INSERT       │
│ statements, which can lead to errors. The    │
│ problem stems from how Django handles        │
│ default values for primary keys,             │
│ particularly when using UUIDField with a     │
│ default value of uuid4. The intention seems  │
│ to be to avoid the double INSERT if an       │
│ explicit primary key is provided. The ticket │
│ suggests a more backwards-compatible         │
│ behavior would be preferable. The fix        │
│ involves ensuring that if an explicit        │
│ primary key is provided, Django only         │
│ performs a single INSERT operation.          │
│                                              │
│ Patch:                                       │
│                                              │
│ ┏━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━┓ │
