│                                              │
│ The core issue is that Django 3.0 introduced │
│ a change in how it handles saving model      │
│ instances with explicit primary keys. In     │
│ Django 2.2 and earlier, saving an instance   │
│ with an explicit primary key would result in │
│ an INSERT followed by an UPDATE. However, in │
│ Django 3.0, it resulted in two INSERT        │
│ statements, which could lead to errors if    │
│ the database didn't support multiple inserts │
│ for the same key. The ticket suggests the    │
│ intention was to preserve the single INSERT  │
│ behavior when an explicit primary key is     │
│ provided.  The loaddata command also suffers │
│ from this change when loading fixtures with  │
│ explicit primary keys.                       │
│                                              │
│ The provided code snippet focuses on the     │
│ Sample model, which has a UUIDField as its   │
│ primary key with a default value of uuid4.   │
│ The problem arises when an explicit primary  │
│ key is provided during instance creation.    │
│                                              │
│ The proposed patch aims to revert the        │
│ behavior to the Django 2.2 behavior,         │
│ ensuring that only a single INSERT statement │
│ is executed when an explicit primary key is  │
│ specified. This will maintain backward       │
│ compatibility and prevent potential issues   │
│ with the loaddata command.                   │
│                                              │
│ Patch:                                       │
│                                              │
│                                              │
│  # modification 1                            │
│  [file]models/models.py[/file]               │
│  [original]                                  │
│  class Sample(models.Model):                 │
│      id = models.UUIDField(primary_key=True  │
