│ Based on the previous analysis, the core     │
│ issue lies in how Django handles primary key │
│ assignments when saving a Sample model       │
│ instance with an explicit pk value,          │
│ particularly in Django 3.0. The problem is   │
│ that it’s attempting to update an existing   │
│ row instead of inserting a new one when an   │
│ explicit pk is provided.                     │
│                                              │
│ Reasoning:                                   │
│                                              │
│ The Sample model’s save() method needs to be │
│ modified to correctly handle both cases:     │
│ when a primary key is explicitly provided    │
│ and when it’s not.  If an explicit pk is     │
│ given, the code should update the existing   │
│ row. If no pk is provided, it should insert  │
│ a new row. The current implementation        │
│ appears to be incorrectly inserting a        │
│ duplicate row when an explicit pk is set.    │
│                                              │
│ Here’s the patch:                            │
│                                              │
│ ┏━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━┓ │
